{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "P1A",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §§9.3, 9.7–9.8 concern score consistency, reinterpreted here as repeatability for a fixed AI. One score and missing execution history establish neither repeat testing nor instability; missing evidence is not an inadequate result.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "gives no execution history",
          "reason": "The material does not establish whether repeated evaluations occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "one headline score of 75%",
          "reason": "A single reported score provides no evidence of variation across identical evaluations."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P1B",
      "instrument_rationale": "Under EFPA §§9.3, 9.7–9.8, repeated-run precision remains unestablished. The explicit absence of repeats contradicts a claim that repeat testing occurred but does not demonstrate unstable scores.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "no repeat evaluations were conducted",
          "reason": "The execution record explicitly rules out repeated evaluations."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "this was the only evaluation performed",
          "reason": "Without repeated evaluations, the findings cannot demonstrate instability across runs."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2A",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §§9.3 and 9.7 support examining repeatability under specified conditions. The observed two-percentage-point range satisfies the report's rule; that rule is not an EFPA reliability coefficient or rating.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "with identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "Six evaluations explicitly used the same conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "73, 74, 74, 75, 74 and 73 percent",
          "reason": "The range is two percentage points, within the stated maximum of three."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2B",
      "instrument_rationale": "Under EFPA §§9.3 and 9.7, the repeated runs supply direct consistency evidence. Their 31-percentage-point range fails the stated rule, supporting instability for this comparison without establishing broader reliability properties.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "with identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "The repeated evaluations explicitly held conditions constant."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "51, 66, 79, 55, 82 and 62 percent",
          "reason": "The range is 31 percentage points, exceeding the stated maximum of three."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3A",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §6.2 distinguishes documentation quality from instrument quality. Task and scoring descriptions do not establish repeatability (§9) or answer errors (§§6.1.3.2–6.1.3.3, 6.3.2).",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It gives no execution history",
          "reason": "Repeated identical-condition evaluations are not documented."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "no findings about mistakes in the provided answers",
          "reason": "The material supplies no evidence demonstrating answer mistakes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3B",
      "instrument_rationale": "The appendix adds technical instructions relevant to EFPA §6.3.8. Under §6.2's distinction between documentation and instrument evidence, it does not resolve missing repeatability evidence (§9) or answer-quality evidence (§6.1).",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It reports no further evaluation results",
          "reason": "Installation information adds no evidence of repeated identical-condition evaluations."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "no further evaluation results or examination of answers",
          "reason": "The appendix provides no findings demonstrating mistakes in supplied answers."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4A",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §§6.1.3.2–6.1.3.3 distinguish content review procedures from their results. Editorial review supports a development check, but correctness is unestablished. Reconciled notes do not supply an agreement estimate; §9.6 applies only if judgemental scoring is involved.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release.",
          "reason": "Both question content and supplied answers underwent editorial examination during development."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "does not state whether mistakes were found or how many remained",
          "reason": "Review procedures alone do not establish that all released answers are correct."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4B",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §§6.1.3.2–6.1.3.3 and 6.3.2 distinguish development checks from content and scoring correctness. The passage documents review and actual released-answer errors; it still provides no pre-reconciliation agreement estimate.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release.",
          "reason": "The development process included examination of both questions and answers."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "identifies twelve supplied answers as wrong in the released version",
          "reason": "Explicit released-answer errors contradict universal correctness."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5A",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §6.3.2 requires transparency about automated scoring correctness. Deterministic marks demonstrate consistency for identical text, not correctness. Under §9.6, inter-rater reliability is inapplicable to scoring without judgemental processes.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No comparison of those marks with independently established correct marks is reported.",
          "reason": "No reported comparison establishes that such testing occurred; unreported does not mean unperformed."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "identical text always receives the same mark",
          "reason": "Consistent scoring does not establish correct scoring for every possible response."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5B",
      "instrument_rationale": "Under EFPA §6.3.2, independent scoring checks provide correctness evidence distinct from consistency. The unresolved discrepancies conflict with universal correctness, and checking 20 chosen responses cannot establish coverage of every response.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "tested the software against independently established correct marks for 20 chosen responses",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly documents independent-reference testing."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Two marks differed and the discrepancies remain unresolved.",
          "reason": "Two scores disagree with independently established correct marks, contradicting universal correctness."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6A",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §10 evaluates validity for the specific interpretation and use. The counts support this narrowly stated comparison. Section 8.3.2.2 permits a norms exception for explicitly restricted raw-score ranking; no broader generalisation follows.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "The stated use is identifying which system solved more of these 48 grid problems in this evaluation.",
          "reason": "The reported counts directly answer that restricted question."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24.",
          "reason": "A solved more of the evaluated problems under the reported fixed conditions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6B",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §§10.1–10.5 require evidence appropriate to the breadth of the intended interpretation. Results on these grid problems establish the local comparison but do not establish general intelligence across subjects and settings.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No results from other tasks or settings are supplied.",
          "reason": "The evidence does not establish sufficiency for the stated general-intelligence use."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24.",
          "reason": "The broader intended use does not change the reported local performance comparison."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7A",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §§3.2, 6.1.3.1, 6.3.2 and 10 relate scoring design to its intended interpretation. Exact-grid scoring matches the explicit requirement; partial-credit scoring is outside this stated comparison.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "The stated scoring requirement is to count only completely correct output grids",
          "reason": "One point for a completely correct grid and zero otherwise implements that requirement."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "partial correctness is deliberately outside this comparison",
          "reason": "Giving zero for partial correctness follows the stated exclusion."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7B",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §§6.1.3.1, 6.3.2 and 10 require scoring to support the intended interpretation. Whole-grid scoring conflicts with the explicitly required cell-level credit, independently of whether scoring is repeatable.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "give a response credit for every correctly completed cell, including partially correct output grids",
          "reason": "The rule awards zero to partially correct grids instead of crediting their correct cells."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A partially correct grid receives zero.",
          "reason": "Zero credit conflicts with the requirement to award credit for correctly completed cells."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8A",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §§2.8, 6.2.4, 9.3 and 9.7 require attention to administration conditions. Instruction variation is reinterpreted as an AI administration manipulation: this examines instruction sensitivity rather than identical-condition repeatability.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "No evaluation repeats an identical instruction.",
          "reason": "Instructions differ, so the evaluations do not have identical conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "each evaluation uses a differently worded instruction",
          "reason": "The study reports score changes while varying instructions and holding the other listed conditions fixed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8B",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §§9.3 and 9.7 apply to repeatability, reinterpreted as score consistency for the same AI under fixed administration conditions. This design establishes repeat testing, but no numerical stability conclusion or instruction-change effect is supplied.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "The software, resources, problem order, settings and instruction are identical on every evaluation.",
          "reason": "The study reports score changes across evaluations with identical stated conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "No different instruction or presentation is studied.",
          "reason": "The study explicitly excludes instruction variation."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "The request for the complete EFPA model is read together with the explicit allowance that full per-case reviews are unnecessary: relevant original criteria are applied, without replacing the instrument. AI systems replace human test takers; repeated runs replace repeated administrations; supplied answers and software marks are considered under content development and scoring checks. Human-specific questions about age, sensory or motor abilities, clinical diagnosis, personal feedback and human qualifications are inapplicable as written; relevant AI access conditions or stakeholder guidance would require explicit reinterpretation. Other Part A descriptors, materials, norms, reliability methods, validity sources, fair-use support, digital reports and Part C benchmark references remain unassessed where the passages supply no relevant evidence; silence does not establish inapplicability or poor quality. No reviewer identity is assigned. Claim labels are not EFPA ratings; if ratings were required, insufficient evidence would generally mean 0, not 1. No aggregate quality score is calculated. Source: EFPA Test Review Model, version 5.0 (2025), supplied extraction, https://www.efpa.eu/, CC BY-SA 4.0. No external material was consulted, and this review implies no EFPA endorsement."
}
